we've somewhat already discussed the  topic of divine law and the pragmatic  and unbiased approach taken by thermos  when creating the laws that mankind and  the gods governed by but today we'll be  focusing on the goddess nemesis and her  drastically different view on punishment  and retribution nemesis was the goddess  that would enact retribution against  those who performed evil deeds or whom  she perceived as heaven undeserved good  fortune like so many of the most ancient  deities within Greek mythology nemesis  was just one of the many children of NYX  and although she was mostly depicted as  having no father there are those who  believe that her father could have been  anyone from Oceanus to Erebus to perhaps  even Zeus nemesis went by several names  including RAM Lucia the goddess of ramus  an ancient coastal city in Greece and  perhaps the most fitting atlas Thea  meaning the inescapable we can assume  referring to her inescapable wrath and  retribution the named nemesis itself was  thought to mean she who distributes or  deals out once again referring to her  distribution of punishment justice and  retribution the children of nemesis are  a fairly debated topic Helen of Troy is  widely regarded to be the daughter of  Zeus and the mortal woman leader but  there are tales where it was instead  nemesis that was considered Helens  mother in a last-ditch attempt to avoid  the advances of Zeus nemesis turned  herself into an animal that animals a  goose but Zeus been the ever persistent  God that he was transformed himself into  a swan and mated of nemesis nemesis in  this bird form would lay an egg an egg  that will be later found by a shepherd  who in turn will to give this egg to  leader leader would keep this egg in a  chest until it hatched and when it did  she would raise her love Troy and assume  the role of her mother  nemesis was seen as being able to  measure one's happiness she could see  whether that happiness was too frequent  or too excessive and if she deemed that  in some way this happiness was  undeserved she would bring loss and  suffering to even up the scales there's  no doubt that the actions of Nemesis  still appeared to be somewhat calculated  but nowhere near the level of thermus  nemesis was still known to act swiftly  with no pity or mercy we can see nemesis  as directing human affairs in a way that  maintains the equilibrium those  exploiting others or shown excessive  hubris towards the gods would be very  quickly dealt with often falling back in  line she was particularly known to  concern herself with matters of love and  she makes an appearance in the famous  story of echo and narcissus narcissus  was extremely beautiful but along with  his beauty he was also extremely  arrogant hating all those that dare to  fall in love with him this arrogance  angered nemesis and so she lured him  into a pool where he could see his own  reflection in the river and upon doing  so he felt instantly in love with the  image he saw not knowing that was in  fact his own unable to leave the beauty  that was his own reflection he would  eventually die  the beauty that narcissus was gifted was  seen by nemesis as undeserved and the  way he treated those who dare to fall in  love with him was enough for the goddess  to intervene and in her punishment  eventually caused his death nemesis  would often be depicted as a winged  goddess undoubtedly because it would  make escape in her pursuit almost  impossible the ancient Greek poet  Messner madhi's wrote a hymn to nemesis  where he addressed her as nemesis winged  balancer of life dark faced goddess  daughter of Justice she was known to  carry with her a sword a lash in a  dagger to enact punishment but she would  also carry scales measuring rod and a  bridle that she would sometimes use to  ride her griffin drawn chariot  nemesis was a deity in Greek mythology  that almost had free rein to do as she  pleased the gods weren't really known to  punish mortals unless it 